      {
        "id": "p1",
        "heading": "Practice -ar",
        "body": "¿Qué aprendiste ayer sobre esta misma materia?\n\nEscribe aquí:\n____________________\n____________________\n____________________\n\n## Repaso\n\nSpanish families: -ar (cantar), -er (comer), -ir (vivir).\n\nConjugate for yo, tú, and él in the present.\n\nExplain one idea with your own words.\n\nStay with the **-ar** family today. Remember: verb = action; conjugate = change for who; stem = fixed part; ending = tip. With **cantar**, drop **-ar** to keep **cant-**, then add **-o / -as / -a**.\n\nMira este ejemplo. Build a tiny story: yo **canto** in the morning, tú **cantas** at school, él **canta** at home. Switch the verb to **hablar** or **bailar** without changing the endings pattern.\n\nPractice: Now it's your turn. Your turn. Write six forms: cantar and hablar for yo, tú, and él. Underline each ending. Draw one clear symbol or small scene about “Spanish verbs: -ar, -er, and -ir”. Add one label from today's lesson.\n\nCommon mistake: Leaving -ar on the verb (yo cantaro). Always drop the infinitive ending first.\n\n## Drawing for the image band\nDraw one clear symbol or small scene about “Spanish verbs: -ar, -er, and -ir”. Add one label from today's lesson."
      }
    ],
    "summary": "In this lesson, you will use Spanish verb endings to say who does an action.\n\n## Drawing for the image band\nDraw one clear symbol or small scene about “Spanish verbs: -ar, -er, and -ir”. Add one label from today's lesson.",
    "memoryPhrase": "Spanish families: **-ar** (cantar → canto, cantas, canta), **-er** (comer → como, comes, come), **-ir** (vivir → vivo, vives, vive).",
